\title{ACTS-Attack: Demonstrated Capabilities and Boundaries\\in Agent-Executed Cryptographic Attacks}
\author{}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
How strong is the retained evidence for language-model security agents
attacking real cryptographic implementations, and what do recorded executions
demonstrate? We study four implementations (RC4, ChaCha20, AES-CBC, ECDSA)
under recorded prompts, tools, routes and oracle contracts, and report both
the demonstrated outcomes and the evidence that bounds them. In a prospective
12-session cohort, agents construct and submit accepted attack candidates in
8/12 sessions, including 3/3 CBC and 3/3 ECDSA sessions; fixed
supplied-operation controls pass 12/12 task oracles; and a 45-handshake
protocol-policy matrix separates permitted classical fallback from required
hybrid behavior. Historical runs retain key recoveries in 10/23 conditional
and 10/51 operational attempts under separately defined populations, and a
controlled side-channel arm attains complete sixteen-byte key recovery over
200,000 samples. The submitted candidate in one escrow key-recovery session
matches its pre-session secret commitment; the same audit corrects metadata
that disagrees with these outcomes: the recorded verifier hash identifies an
older oracle, none of the eight successful cohort durations has a raw
endpoint pair, and generated code runs on the host without enforced secret
isolation. Across six public releases, a release-level investigation
separates evaluator behavior and source-level answer flow from published-run
attribution, and an eight-proposition replay over 35 pinned files makes each
correction inspectable. The result is a task-specific capability baseline for
agent-executed cryptographic attacks together with a claim-level evidence
methodology: verified recoveries and corrected interpretations remain visible
together, each linked to inspectable records.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
protocol security, cryptographic implementations, side channels, benchmark validity, execution provenance, reproducibility
\end{IEEEkeywords}

